MAJOR_VERSION=4
STANDARD_VERSION=2
RELEASE_VERSION=0
PRERELEASE=1
DEV_VERSION=8
DESCRIPTION<<VER_END
PRE 4.2.0 (P3-13 server foundations): branch cut from develop to carry the server work (#239) and the deferred-item promotion tracks (#279 Wi-Fi, #280 IPv6). This train lands the colour-coded server / client foundations on top of the network-prep train. Contract shard `contracts/networking/contract_p3_server.h` (FL_CONTRACT_P3_NETWORKING_REV bumped to 11; shard REV bumped to 2): server / client / announcement vocabulary, host dual-role model, member registry with `{N}` disambiguation, host-global nick rules (reject when nick equals another connected member's principal or nick), peer-IP tracking, four push opcodes JOIN_ANNOUNCE/LEAVE_ANNOUNCE/NICK_SET_ANNOUNCE/SERVER_ANNOUNCE (0x05..0x08), private-message opcodes OP_MSG_DIRECT/OP_MSG_DIRECT_DELIVER (0x12/0x13), roster-snapshot OP_MEMBER_LIST_SNAPSHOT (0x09), and an extended payload encoding for the existing OP_CTRL_HOST_PROMOTE (0x22) carrying `[new_host_id][new_host_ip_be][new_host_port]`. Implementation: `kernel/core/net/net_server.[ch]` (HELLO/HELLO_ACK, optional NICK_PROMPT on principal collision, JOIN_ANNOUNCE then NICK announcement per the sequencing rule, MSG_BROADCAST relay, `fl_net_server_send_public`, `fl_net_server_send_private` host -> recipient route, `fl_net_server_broadcast_member_list` after every join/leave/nick, `fl_net_server_set_host_nick` with full collision check, `fl_net_server_announce`, ordered LEAVE_ANNOUNCE on drop, `fl_net_server_transfer_and_stop` selects lowest non-host id and broadcasts CTRL_HOST_PROMOTE with `[id][ip_be][port]` before tearing down); `kernel/core/net/net_client.[ch]` (state machine DISCONNECTED->CONNECTING->CONNECTED->DISCONNECTING, own-IP tracking via getsockname(), `fl_net_client_connect_from` to source from a specific local IP, `fl_net_client_send_private`, cached roster snapshot from MEMBER_LIST_SNAPSHOT with local-only nicks, `fl_net_client_member_lookup` honouring host-global nick > local-only nick > principal+disambig with collision detection, `fl_net_client_poll` decodes every announce opcode + OP_ERR + MSG_DIRECT_DELIVER + MEMBER_LIST + HOST_PROMOTE into events); `kernel/core/net/server_bg.[ch]` (hosted-only pthread loops; FL_RESULT_NOSYS off-host); `kernel/core/net/net_socket.c` adds `fl_net_sock_connect_from` (optional local-IP bind before connect), `fl_net_sock_peer_ipv4`, `fl_net_sock_local_ipv4`, and SO_REUSEADDR on every bind so demos do not race TIME_WAIT. Shell layer: `userland/shell/fl_colors.[ch]` rewritten to KNRM/KRED/KGRN/KYEL/KBLU/KMAG/KCYN/KWHT macros per the stackoverflow.com/a/3586005 CC BY-SA 2.5 attribution (retrieved 2026-05-29), adds prompt-aware prelude/postlude hooks, adds CYN `[Server Message, You -> User]:`, `[Server Message, User -> You]:`, `[Server Message, You -> All]:`, `[Server Message, From User]:` helpers, and an interactive `fl_color_prompt_yellow` that does not deadlock the lock used by the async helpers. New `userland/shell/shell_io.[ch]` owns a pthread mutex around stdout plus a snapshot of the readline buffer; `userland/shell/interpreter.c` calls `fl_shell_io_set_prompt_active(1/0, line, len, pos)` around every input read so background server / client receive threads can clear the live `shell> ...` prompt, print their tagged line, and redraw the buffer (no more "shell> [Server Announcement] ..." glue). `userland/command/cmd_server.c` rewritten: verbs `host`, `join` (with optional `-bind <local_ip>`), `msg [-all|-user <name> [-id <N>]|-id <N>] <text>` (private vs global routing with CYN render), `announce` (host only, BLU), `nick [-local] -id <N> -name <nick>`, `connected` (anyone can run; client reads from cached snapshot with a `(you)` marker), `leave` (host triggers transfer-and-stop, client triggers disconnect), `kill` (host only). Joining a server that triggers NICK_PROMPT now runs a synchronous yellow Y/N + nick-line prompt before the background loop starts. A detached pthread spawned from the `EVENT_HOST_PROMOTE` callback automatically takes over as the new host (chosen successor) or reconnects to the new host (other peers) with a 10x150 ms back-off so the new listener has time to bind. `userland/command/cmd_exit.c` calls a new `cmd_server_atexit()` so `exit [-y/-n]` always leaves / transfers the session before the shell dies. `userland/shell/sh.c` now registers the prompt-aware hooks at startup. Tests: `tests/test_p3_server.c` extended to four sub-tests (announce/join/leave/nick; private+public messages with host-global nick overriding local-only nick; leave restores singleton principal; host transfer picks lowest non-host id); `tests/test_invariants.c` updated for the bumped networking shard revision; new `tests/manual_demo_multi_ip.sh` drives a four-pane tmux session (host on `10.99.0.1`, clients sourced from `10.99.0.10/.20/.30` loopback aliases — NOT `127.0.0.1`), exercises join, Y/N nick prompt, public + private chat, `server connected` from both host and client, in-session `whoami`, and host transfer with automatic reconnect; outputs are captured to `/opt/cursor/artifacts/demo_multi_ip_{host,A,B,C}.{ansi,txt}` plus a rendered PNG. `tests/manual_demo_server_foundations.sh` (older 3-pane loopback demo) retained for regression. `Makefile` adds `net_server.c`, `net_client.c`, `server_bg.c`, `userland/shell/fl_colors.c` to NET_CORE_SRCS and `userland/shell/shell_io.c` to SHELL_SRCS; new `make test_p3_server` target. `docs/SERVER.md` updated: KCYN/KBLU/KGRN/KRED/KYEL/KWHT palette table with stackoverflow attribution, prompt-aware async print note, new sections 3.3 Private and public messages, 3.4 Multi-IP / non-loopback hosting, 3.5 Host transfer on leave/exit, 3.6 Shell exit integration; older `server send -file` section retained as 3.7 (planned). Follow-up corrections from the user (latest commit): (1) Host transfer is now a BLUE Server Announcement `[Server Announcement]: <display> is now the host` broadcast to every peer by `fl_net_server_transfer_and_stop()` BEFORE tear-down (replaces the per-user cyan `[SERVER]: You are now the host.` DM); the new host always reindexes to `member_id 1` by virtue of starting a fresh `fl_net_server_host_start()` (stack-style reindex for the auto path; the future "host explicitly selects successor" path will extend the map instead). The local `[Server] host transferred to member_id N` line on the OLD host is dropped — the broadcast announce covers it. (2) Colon added after the tag — every blue announce now reads `[Server Announcement]: <text>` (was `[Server Announcement] <text>`); `userland/shell/fl_colors.c` updated. (3) Reduce duplicate render: server now skips the sender when fanning out `OP_MSG_BROADCAST` via a new `broadcast_to_peers_except()` helper, AND `cmd_server.c verb_msg` no longer prints the local `[Server Message, You -> All]: ...` / `[Server Message, You -> <user>]: ...` confirmation line — silence is success, failures still surface as `[ERROR]`. The host's own `fl_net_server_send_public` also skips the host self-member so the host's terminal does not echo its own broadcast. (4) Fix CUnit link on x86-64 GAS (CI failure): `userland/shell/shell_io.c` added to `TEST_SRCS` so `BPForbes_Flinstone_Tests` resolves `fl_shell_io_lock` / `fl_shell_io_unlock` referenced by `cmd_server.o`. (5) `tests/test_p3_server.c` strengthened with a "no echo back to sender" assertion in `test_messages_and_roster` — Jack's `logJack.msgs` counter must stay unchanged after Jack broadcasts; Jill's last_msg still contains "Jack: Hi everyone". Previous iteration kept: `whoami` after a nicked user leaves the server still demonstrated in `tests/manual_demo_multi_ip.sh` (Bobby leaves, runs `whoami`, gets `flinstone`); structural assertion in `test_p3_server.c` that `cJill.principal == "Jill"` is unchanged after `fl_net_server_set_host_nick(jill, "Boss")`.

Cross-subnet packet-capture demo (this commit): new `tests/manual_demo_netns_pcap.sh` brings up a routed two-subnet topology entirely in network namespaces (192.168.10.0/24 ↔ fl_router with `ip_forward=1` ↔ 192.168.20.0/24) and runs `tcpdump` inside the router namespace so every TCP segment crossing the subnet boundary is captured. Produces `netns_router_capture.pcap` (Wireshark-openable), `netns_router_capture.txt` (tcpdump timeline), and `netns_router_session_frames.txt` (per-frame decode via `tests/decode_session_pcap.py`, a new standalone scapy-based decoder that walks the session header — magic 0x46, version, opcode → name from `contract_p3_session_wire.h`, flags, length, ASCII preview, plus per-opcode counts). New `make test_netns_pcap` target is gated by `FL_NETNS_PCAP_OK=1` so the default test sweep does not require sudo / netns. `AGENTS.md` Cursor Cloud one-liner now installs `iproute2` and `tcpdump` and documents the `bridge-nf-call-iptables=0` sysctl plus the mininet-vs-raw-netns trade-off (mininet's OVS backend needs a loadable kernel module unavailable inside containerised CI). `docs/SERVER.md` gains a new §3.4 "Packet capture (cross-subnet end-to-end evidence)" subsection; subsequent sections renumbered 3.5–3.8. Earlier iterations kept: foreground sync nick prompt during `server join` wraps the Y/N + nick-line read in `fl_shell_io_lock/unlock`, and auto-reconnect on host transfer surfaces a NICK_PROMPT as a cyan `[SERVER]: ...` DM (with `server set-nick <name>` verb) instead of a sync prompt from a background thread. #279 (Wi-Fi station) and #280 (IPv6+ICMPv6+NDP) remain scoped on this branch for follow-up commits per the user's "later once the server is more polished" guidance. CI fix: `fl_net_server_accept_pending` HELLO_ACK rollback called `recompute_disambig(srv, principal_copy)` without a local `principal_copy` — use `m->principal` (member slot still holds principal after rollback) so `make ARCH=x86_64_gas CHANGELOG_CI=1` builds.
VER_END

RELEASE_DATE=2026-05-28
